    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "1. **Understand the problem:** We need to find a two-digit number \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\times t \\) are 36. In other words, we need \\( 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100} \\).\n\n2. **Express the congruence:**\n   \\[\n   11t = 100k + 36 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k.\n   \\]\n   This can be rewritten as:\n   \\[\n   11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Find the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100:**\n   We need to find an integer \\( x \\) such that:\n   \\[\n   11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}.\n   \\]\n   This can be solved using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm or by checking values.\n\n   - Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm, we find:\n     \\[\n     11 \\times 89 = 1 \\pmod{100}.\n     \\]\n     Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 89.\n\n4. **Multiply both sides of the congruence by 89:**\n   \\[\n   11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n   \\]\n   Multiplying both sides by 89:\n   \\[\n   t \\equiv 36 \\times 89 \\pmod{100}.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate \\( 36 \\times 89 \\mod 100 \\):**\n   \\[\n   36 \\times 89 = 3204.\n   \\]\n   Taking modulo 100:\n   \\[\n   3204 \\mod 100 = 4.\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   t \\equiv 4 \\pmod{100}.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Formulate the unique two-digit integer \\( t \\):**\n   Since \\( t \\) is a two-digit number and must satisfy \\( t \\equiv 4 \\pmod{100} \\), the unique solution is:\n   \\[\n   t = 4.\n   \\]\n\nThe unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
